't know, but it is quite short, just 13 episodes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Spoken like a &quot;spoiled American&quot;. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Thirteen or so episodes is increasingly becoming the norm for many programs -- particularly internet-run and/or cable shows -- as an endeavor towards having quality-over-quantity. The &quot;U.S. Standard&quot; 22 per season is considered a luxury for most non-US productions and/or internet or cable produced shows; it's ultimately more cost effective to do 12-14 episodes and simply up the game in terms of writing and actor chemistry. Arrow or Supernatural may run 22-a-year because that's what the US expects and that's what the Hollywood TV system operates on, but &quot;13-and-go&quot; is not a bad thing, at all. Hell, &quot;Peeky Blinders&quot; is *EXCELLENT* -- a great period show with a fantastic cast... and they only do 6 episodes per season (though the episodes are about a full, proper hour long, vs. the US standard 42 minutes w/ commercials, so it's more like 8 episodes of material, give or take). A great example of quality-over-quantity. I wouldn't *want* PB to be a full 22 episodes a season -- they'd fuck it up. Treating it the way they do focuses and distills the quality of the program over a bulk presentation. DD is a similar example: an excellent show that could very easily get fucked-up if made to &quot;fill&quot; a conventional US season/episode order.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{